nitial Conditions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050098 #edb/ref 1033 2088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050098 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "2d131eff-4143-5f33-ab1b-d2795c01303f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050309 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050309 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050309 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050309 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050309 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050309 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050309 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving Homogeneous Systems of ODEs With Complex Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050309 #edb/ref 1033 2819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050309 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "3ece60c2-2440-5884-ade0-5ec738e1db78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving Inhomogeneous Systems of ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1033 3189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "68086256-0558-57ff-bb09-7a45eb02cfce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157069 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7043" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157069 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/23/2376d4518853bf4a71511133c709d5fdabadb3db94c88f941274dd7d9557dd5a.svg)\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n![](images/12/12ba5728836320b3d99d138507e5bb71e7f3a6bd2d93635443b7c26bb71a7ddd.svg)\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/fa/faa6212a47ec98f63248d7de696decaaba0b767e5771eef523ec1e9373051f21.svg)*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157069 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a284986-5300-5bad-a33c-aa2bc47712b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157069 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/d3/d33b2b3ad04f460f27c6f199b9f54ae327f48a96d1b3de8ce270e677440cac74.svg) \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/9d/9d5190da8a9a59e2691c8ab16e366be20a14c6c7ce5b8f57986d4faa50cceb9e.svg)\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1$ is \n$\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n![](images/61/61c5ce70ef3272667953b10eb895ba8c480723399d5aeb96eae0e0f54064c1e1.svg)\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t) = \\cos{t} + \\sin{t}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157070 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7044" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157070 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n![](images/0c/0c7d4c9f4782665ddfa9e36fa52957f308e49d6e42e75f94bb01fc998bbce219.svg)\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 2\\textrm{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/ae/ae46b58d986d4c703470adb45128c046e6e0e572ce92593a0661e990bcb1b661.svg)*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157070 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8e797f4b-fe53-5d0a-a4b0-8fb51ee04c92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157070 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/ea/eaa7fa7c58b91792b295e853a01912a5ec45edda30f5de68f5e3952a73fae014.svg)\n\nWe are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 2\\textrm{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/a2/a290109e5eb086926e15a1cffa9e11dd54b8843b3891068af7c322d10baa20de.svg)\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1$ is \n$\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 2\\textrm{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n![](images/1e/1ec89bb86150c8b5002835d1f7a8f796f514e8c05f70303c833d13e922d5d390.svg)\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}](\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition ![](images/b2/b263aa33a22f008a8cc114136e985b5655cd0f097584c48311bb28eca256c2d2.svg) Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =1[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157071 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7045" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157071 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7a/7a0372520a2b91d2e3f928f808d210700f1076e44b94900244cc4af78e28cd24.svg)\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n![](images/e5/e5e413f7e2bea547939d4aa4cb2d99206750e5992543cd83ebde713427bc5871.svg)\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is $\\lambda_1=1+\\textrm{i}.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157071 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53243f33-77a1-59f1-bce9-3f084e087593" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157071 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/b2/b2099bd8583863c7d0489160094e0a47a32d5eb8921ca711d927056dd7d400ce.svg) \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda_1=1+\\textrm{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For $\\lambda_1 =1+\\textrm{i},$ we get \n![](images/40/4089109e1f91de1a63e8a8c7c820394cc9dc7904d15c7d45bdc58e35fc695c4d.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/43/43d0d264f3ea97d5a7a14ac13ea56963840bfacc002275e66ec53439652d42d7.svg)\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1$ is \n$\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 1+\\textrm{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n![](images/3e/3e20df8aa735d7f95baa39fc294f1ceacddfa0e0db922c8a4d1ef0ade5f2a405.svg)\n\nWe now separate this solution into its real and imaginary parts. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(1+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ } \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t) = \\cos{t}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157072 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7046" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157072 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n![](images/ea/ea0084a8b024c1b1680e34a3ff3d395f166de655db503bd3f9415c23be24a5f1.svg)\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is $\\lambda_1=3+\\textrm{i}.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157072 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9bd3af1e-e569-5cf1-8c4e-97b9ecc35174" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157072 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/e4/e49a5731becdf1c7c11904a9da6407a9ef75e3af91e278590ececdab49b618c7.svg) \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda_1=3+\\textrm{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For $\\lambda_1 =3+\\textrm{i},$ we get \n![](images/fb/fba3794fb2b0767d7f221dc766636f51a1ffe06fd529ab1423df74aeae2b9db6.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/6e/6e16f0e74bf298c51302325450383ff3edf67912994c6604d22a3bbca1c292bd.svg)\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1$ is \n$\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 3 + \\textrm{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n![](images/43/43419fd3567cb18d8132bdc6e12a204efe946280f83593056394d588b07cc71b.svg)\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(3+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}({e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition ![](images/f6/f61dd79b0a32acd6788f4f4f27e7438c52612b59266145af08b79d38687dd170.svg) Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =1[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-3[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157448 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157448 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157448 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157448 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157448 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157448 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157448 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157448 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157448 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157448 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157448 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9e3dd935-2058-5b28-bdbb-0c45586730d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157449