17592186045417] [17592186157741 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157741 #edb/ref 1069 "q-328592" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157741 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157741 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157741 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "284bef67-027d-5606-82aa-6c0a65dd3c2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157741 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158396 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158396 #edb/ref 1061 577657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158396 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "24b6ab49-7a81-5a3b-a140-aabc60a9f348" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158396 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158397 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158397 #edb/ref 1061 577658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158397 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "33bad08d-6b45-5628-aba5-8acb1bac5825" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158397 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158398 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158398 #edb/ref 1061 577661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158398 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9044a6cf-4cf6-55f0-81fe-5f88ad0b36be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158398 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158399 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158399 #edb/ref 1061 577664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158399 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "671c8d98-22a1-54a0-98cc-259902e11176" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158399 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158400 #edb/ref 1061 577667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158400 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "911f4d76-3073-51d2-8506-25330ab817ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158400 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158469 #edb/ref 1064 "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\n \n\nwe know that if $A$ has distinct eigenvalues, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda_2} t}.\n$$\n\nThis formula works even when the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1, \\lambda_2$ are complex. However, using it directly requires working with complex exponentials, which can be cumbersome.\n\nA more direct method is to find just *one* of the complex solutions, say $\\mathbf x_1(t) = \\mathbf{v}_1 e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda_1} t},$ and separate it into its *real and imaginary parts*.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t} \\\\  & ={{{w}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Real Part}}\\,+\\,\\text{i}\\,\\,\\,{{{w}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Imaginary Part}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general *real-valued* solution is then a linear combination of these two parts:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x(t) = c_1 \\mathbf w_1(t) + c_2 \\mathbf w_2(t)\n$$\n\n**Note:** This method may seem surprising. Why can we construct the general solution using only *one* of the complex solutions, $\\mathbf x_1(t)?$\n\nThe reason is that the second solution, $\\mathbf x_2(t),$ doesn't provide any new information. For a real matrix $A,$ the complex eigenvalues and eigenvectors come in *conjugate pairs*, which means $\\mathbf x_2(t)$ is simply the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf x_1(t).$\n\nSince $\\mathbf x_2(t) = \\mathbf w_1 (t) - \\textrm{i} \\, \\mathbf w_2(t),$ it is also a linear combination of the real and imaginary parts of $\\mathbf x_1(t).$\n\nTo build our general solution, we just need two *linearly independent* real solutions. The key facts are the following:\n\n- For a system with complex eigenvalues, the real part $\\mathbf w_1(t)$ and the imaginary part $\\mathbf w_2(t)$ are *always* linearly independent.\n\n- Since they are two linearly independent solutions, they can form the basis for the general solution.\n\nSo, we can use them directly and bypass the need to work with $\\mathbf x_2(t)$ at all.\n\nLet's see a concrete example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158469 #edb/ref 1065 4283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158469 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a93ff6d6-1eb0-5e42-8b69-ae73fc891839" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158469 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302815 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302815 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302815 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "39a5dad7-c94f-51da-a677-ef81f5bf32cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302822 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302822 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302822 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3ac8dcd5-8feb-5327-aa14-8382a8c89d1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302950 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+