417] [17592186414133 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186414126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414133 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414133 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414133 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186414125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414133 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bfe17ed2-090f-567a-b946-9998b5018bd8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414134 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186414106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414134 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186414118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414134 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186414119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414134 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186414127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414134 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186414129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414134 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414134 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414134 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186414118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414134 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dc7055a9-5fc1-5c31-8fe0-833c83e126c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414135 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414135 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334220" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414135 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414135 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+4\\text{i}.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414135 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "421de516-3253-5c4d-bb0f-0143da489cf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414135 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+4\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+4\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i} & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+4\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(3+4\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(3+4\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}(2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}({e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414136 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414136 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334102" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414136 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414136 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+\\text{i}.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414136 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a4f7c8d4-fcef-54e6-85b4-aab824651514" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414136 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 1 & -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(3+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(3+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414137 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414137 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334057" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414137 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414137 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414137 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b206ecea-c987-5f98-a428-5bb6722fc6f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414137 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}](\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =-2[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414138 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414138 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333911" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414138 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414138 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414138 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ee5bc47e-f5eb-5ac3-a599-35150942e1b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414138 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414139 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414139 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334217" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414139 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414139 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414139 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ff1d5823-2f0a-574c-b322-55ba80787b34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414139 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-2+3\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-2+3\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}(-{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}({e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
